# App layout

Every app in this repo is one tree. `main.ts` is the `serve()` callback and
boots it.

A **page** is a folder that exports handlers for one path (`Home`, `list`,
`add`). The table that owns that path binds them with
`route("/guestbook", { GET: list, POST: add })`. That table is the `serve()`
callback or a wrap / prefix group's callback. A page module never calls
`route()` or `group()`, and never exports a `{ GET }` bag or a function
named `GET`.

`group()` is only a **prefixed subtree** (nested URLs, or layouts /
middleware / `notFound` on that prefix) or a **pathless wrap**. `group("/")`
is illegal. Pathless `group()` is not the `/` page.

README snippets may inline `serve(callback)` to show the whole flow; those
are not apps.

## File names

Deno snake_case. Feature folders match the path (`docs/` for `/docs`).
Layout and middleware files are `{folder}_{role}`: `menu_layout.tsx`,
`menu_middleware.ts`. Nested `reviews/` uses `reviews_layout.tsx`. At the app
root: `root_layout.tsx`, `logger_middleware.ts`, `errors.tsx`. Client modules
are `*_client.ts` (`clock_client.ts`) and sit beside the file that registers
them. No `routes/` directory, no app `client/` folder, no `_` magic prefixes,
and no `*_route.tsx`.

`errors.tsx` holds the named `notFound`, `error`, and `fatal`
exports. Those are not routes. Root `notFound` / `error` live on the root
callback; a group's `notFound` / `error` live on that group's `mod.ts`.
`fatal` stays on `serve()` options.

Ordinary extra modules (`todos.ts`, later `db.ts`) sit beside the handlers.
No blessed view / service / DAL layer: extract `menu_service.ts` or a
component when needed. No `layouts/` or `middleware/` folders.

## Exports

A component that returns `Element` is PascalCase (`Home`, `RootLayout`,
`ErrorPage`). Middleware and per-method handlers are camelCase (`logger`,
`list`, `add`). Do not export `GET` as the function name. Do not export a
`{ GET: Home }` bag.

`mod.ts` (`mod.tsx` when the file contains JSX) is the page module or the
group: handlers in that file, or `group()` plus child `route()` calls. No
`*_route.tsx` and no `controller.ts` inside a page or group folder.

## Table

`main.ts` is the `serve()` callback. It binds `route()` for every root page
and any root one-offs, then calls `serve`. Prefix and wrap groups call
`route()` for their children. Do not `import { group }` inside a bag
callback. Do not export `route()`.

```tsx
// home/mod.tsx
export function Home(_ctx: Ctx, html: Html) {
  return html(<h1>Hello</h1>);
}
```

```tsx
// guestbook/mod.tsx
export function list(_ctx: Ctx, html: Html) {
  return html(<h1>Guestbook</h1>);
}

export async function add(ctx: Ctx) {
  return Response.redirect(new URL("/guestbook", ctx.url), 303);
}
```

`group("/", …)` is illegal. Pathless `group(({ route }) => …)` is a
layout/middleware shell with no extra prefix. A pre-built `Group` types
`ctx.params` from its own prefix; nested groups do not read ancestor params
they did not declare. Shared data goes on `ctx.state`.

`main.ts` is:

```ts
import { serve } from "dashi";
import { Home } from "./home/mod.tsx";
import { add, list } from "./guestbook/mod.tsx";
import { RootLayout } from "./root_layout.tsx";
import { fatal } from "./errors.tsx";
import { nested } from "./nested/mod.tsx";

if (import.meta.main) {
  serve(({ route }) => ({
    layouts: [RootLayout],
    routes: [
      route("/", { GET: Home }),
      route("/guestbook", { GET: list, POST: add }),
      nested,
    ],
  }), { fatal });
}
```

A prefixed group is a URL subtree. The parent drops that `Group` into
`routes`:

```ts
import { group } from "dashi";
import { NestedLayout } from "./nested_layout.tsx";

export const nested = group("/nested", ({ route }) => ({
  layouts: [NestedLayout],
  routes: [route("/", { GET: Nested })],
}));
```

## Fixtures

Int-tests and e2e fixtures may use a **file** next to the table when the
page is only handlers (`echo.tsx`), and a **folder** when it has page-local
components, a `*_client.ts`, or other tables import that page (`ok/mod.ts`).
If `name.tsx` is already a shared module, the page is `name/mod.tsx` so the
two do not collide. Wrap-owned handlers that are not themselves a root page
live in that group's folder. Examples and real apps always use a folder.

The main fixture (`int-tests/fixtures/app/`) follows that fixture split.
Extra fixtures that cannot share that `serve()` table are a small `main.ts`
or `main.tsx` in their own folder, inline `serve(callback)` harnesses. They
are not workspace members. Fixture `main.ts` exports `start()` for `boot` /
`withBrowser`. The e2e fixtures use the same tree; extra e2e fixtures follow
the same rule.

## Imports

App code imports from `dashi` (`Html`, `Patches`, `Ctx`, `serve`, …).
JSX types (`Element`, `HTMLAttributes`, `DashiNode`, `JSX`) import from
`dashi/jsx-runtime`. Browser APIs import from `dashi/client`. CORS
imports from `dashi/cors`. The published export list lives in the
public-api rule.
